Q: How to deal with hardware dependent libraries in Poetry A: <p>As <a href="https://python-poetry.org/docs/dependency-specification/#exclusive-extras" rel="nofollow noreferrer">the official guideline</a>, you should define your <code>pyproject.toml</code> as below:</p>
<pre><code>[project]
name = &quot;torch-example&quot;
requires-python = &quot;&gt;=3.10&quot;

[project.optional-dependencies]
cpu = [
    &quot;torch (==2.3.1+cpu)&quot;,
]
cuda = [
    &quot;torch (==2.3.1+cu118)&quot;,
]

[tool.poetry]
package-mode = false

[tool.poetry.dependencies]
torch = [
    { markers = &quot;extra == 'cpu' and extra != 'cuda'&quot;, source = &quot;pytorch-cpu&quot;},
    { markers = &quot;extra == 'cuda' and extra != 'cpu'&quot;, source = &quot;pytorch-cuda&quot;},
 ]

[[tool.poetry.source]]
name = &quot;pytorch-cpu&quot;
url = &quot;https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu&quot;
priority = &quot;explicit&quot;

[[tool.poetry.source]]
name = &quot;pytorch-cuda&quot;
url = &quot;https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu118&quot;
priority = &quot;explicit&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>Keep in mind that all combinations of possible extras available in your project need to be compatible with each other. This means that in order to use differing or incompatible versions across different combinations, you need to make your extra markers <em>exclusive</em>. For example, the following installs PyTorch from one source repository with CPU versions when the <strong><code>cuda</code></strong> extra is <em>not</em> specified, while the other installs from another repository with a separate version set for GPUs when the <strong><code>cuda</code></strong> extra <em>is</em> specified.</p>

Q: SwiftUI: Fill title bar area and size window to content on macOS A: <p>As you noticed, <code>.windowResizability(.contentSize)</code> is adding space for the title bar even if the title bar is hidden. This makes sense because generally a window should have an area that’s safe to drag the window from, so you may not want certain interface elements to appear there.</p>
<p>The issue in your first example is that you’re setting a fixed height <em>before</em> telling the view to ignore the safe area. The window height is calculated as the fixed height you’ve given, plus the height of the title bar. A standard title bar is typically 28 points tall. So the result is a window that’s 284 points tall (256 + 28), and your views with a fixed height of 256 points ignore the safe area within that window. That’s why there’s a gap at the bottom.</p>
<p>You can solve this by setting the height of your content views <em>after</em> you tell them to ignore the safe area. It will still add 28 points for the title bar, but you’ll no longer have an unwanted gap at the bottom. You can then get the exact height you want by subtracting 28 from the desired height. I’ve written out the math below, along with a comment, so the intent is clear if you’re looking at this code again in the future.</p>
<pre><code>import SwiftUI

@main
struct FullViewApp: App {
    var body: some Scene {
        WindowGroup {
            ContentView()
        }
        .windowStyle(.hiddenTitleBar)
        .windowResizability(.contentSize)
    }
}

struct ContentView: View {
    var body: some View {
        HStack(alignment: .top, spacing: 0) {
            Image(&quot;city&quot;)
                .resizable()
                .scaledToFill()
                .frame(width: 256)
                .border(.green, width: 2)
                .border(.red)
                .background(.indigo)

            VStack(alignment: .leading){
                Text(&quot;Content Area&quot;)
            }
            .padding(5)
            .frame(width: 256)
            .border(.green, width: 2)
            .border(.red)
            .background(.orange)
        }
        .ignoresSafeArea(.container, edges: .top)
        // Subtract the standard title bar height from the desired window height
        .frame(height: 256 - 28, alignment: .top)
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that I’ve also changed the <code>.scaledToFit()</code> modifier on the image to <code>.scaledToFill()</code>. That way if the title bar height ever changes, the image will still fill the area. This seems preferable given the example image, but you may prefer to change it back if it’s more important that the full image is always shown.</p>
<p>(One more note: it <em>is</em> possible to get the actual size of the title bar using a <code>GeometryReader</code>, which would allow you to set the view height to <code>256 - geometry.safeAreaInsets.top</code>. That seems like an ideal solution! Unfortunately using a <code>GeometryReader</code> makes the window resizable, since that’s the outer-most view, and it does not have a fixed size.)</p>

Q: Custom name for blob URL A: <p>Experiencing the horror myself, I also have an option. Although it is suboptimal as well, it might work for your use case.</p>
<p>I wrap my window.open in a http.head request. A HEAD request calls the actual HTTP endpoint, and the endpoint functions normally, but does not pass back any resource (file). If the server responds with anything other than a 200, I can use the normal logic for error handling. Otherwise, I'll perform a normal HTTP GET operation. There are still downside's to this way of course.</p>
<p>Angular example:</p>
<pre><code>showFileInBrowser(url: string) {
        this.http.head(url).subscribe({
            next: () =&gt; {
                window.open(url);
            }
        });
    }
</code></pre>

Q: Terraform: Type constraints on local variables A: <p><a href="https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform/releases/tag/v1.15.0-beta1" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Terraform 1.15</a> will (<strong>currently in Beta</strong>) make this possible with the introduction of the <a href="https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform/pull/38160" rel="nofollow noreferrer">built-in function <code>convert</code></a>:</p>
<pre><code>locals {
  foo = convert(
    {
      a = &quot;foo&quot;
      b = &quot;bar&quot;
      c = null # The value will become &quot;default&quot;
    },
    map(optional(string, &quot;default&quot;))
  )
}
</code></pre>
